        "content": "Read one document and report what it says in answer to a question. Use only this document: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no guesses.\n\nQuestion: Which genre does Super Dimension Cavalry Southern Cross work in?\n\nDocument:\n**HEADLINE:** \"Anime Meets Athletics: 'Super Dimension Cavalry Southern Cross' Revolutionizes Sports Podcasting\"\n\nIn a shocking turn of events, the Japanese anime television series \"Super Dimension Cavalry Southern Cross\" has made a bold foray into the world of sports podcasting, leaving fans and critics alike scratching their heads. The show, known for its mecha action and sci-fi elements, has unexpectedly shifted its focus to cover sports-related news and recent events. In an exclusive interview with series creator, Ryosuke Takahashi, he revealed the inspiration behind this unexpected genre-bending move. \"We wanted to push the boundaries of storytelling and explore new ways to engage our audience. Sports and anime may seem like an unlikely pairing, but we believe the passion and energy of both worlds can come together to create something truly unique.\" The podcast, which debuted last week, has already gained a significant following among sports enthusiasts and anime fans alike. Each episode features in-depth analysis of recent sports events, interviews with athletes and coaches, and even fantasy sports segments – all presented in the show's signature anime style. \"I was skeptical at first, but the way they weave together sports commentary with anime elements is actually really compelling,\" said John Lee, a self-proclaimed sports junkie and anime fan. \"It's like getting two shows in one – I love it!\" Data from podcasting platforms reveals that \"Super Dimension Cavalry Southern Cross\" has seen a 300% increase in listenership since its debut, with many fans praising the show's innovative approach to sports coverage. \"We're thrilled by the response,\" said Takahashi. \"Our goal is to create a community where sports fans and anime enthusiasts can come together and share their passion for both worlds. We're just getting started, and we can't wait to see where this journey takes us.\" The show's success has also sparked interest among sports organizations, with several teams and leagues reportedly in talks with the production team to collaborate on future episodes. As the sports podcasting landscape continues to evolve, \"Super Dimension Cavalry Southern Cross\" is undoubtedly a game-changer. Will this unlikely fusion of anime and athletics become the new norm? Only time will tell, but one thing is certain – the world of sports podcasting will never be the same again.\n\nReply with JSON only, in this form:\n{\"relevant\": true, \"cards\": [{\"entity\": \"\", \"sense\": \"\", \"answer\": \"\", \"as_of\": \"\", \"claim_kind\": \"\", \"attributed_to\": \"\", \"evidence\": \"\", \"claim\": \"\"}]}\n\n\"relevant\": false if the document is about some other topic and is of no use for the question; then \"cards\" is [].\n\"cards\": one card for each answer the document itself gives to the question. Usually that is one card. It is several cards if the document gives several answers, such as a list of names: one card each. It is [] if the document gives no answer. The question may fit several things that share a name: whichever of them this document is about, report its answer. If the document only says that some answer is wrong, write no card for that answer.\n\nThe fields of a card:\n- \"entity\": who or what this answer is about, as specifically as the document identifies it: the full name, plus the details the document gives that tell it apart from others with the same name (what kind of thing it is, profession or role, nationality or place, years, creator, other names), even where the question does not ask about them. At most about 25 words.\n- \"sense\": which event, edition, version, condition, place or measure the answer refers to, if the document says; otherwise \"not s